{"Channel": {"name": "bug-davidgarcia-WorkFlowGenie", "channelID": "ch-genie-f3ea48"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_e3c15ff5", "timestamp": "2026-09-01T00:14:00", "text": "Let's get these bugs squashed! 💪", "utterranceID": "20260831-10-ec3d6"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260831-10-ec3d6"}
{"Channel": {"name": "bug-davidgarcia-WorkFlowGenie", "channelID": "ch-genie-f3ea48"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_686130c8", "timestamp": "2026-09-02T14:38:00", "text": "Hi team, the bug about incorrect event prioritization, titled 'Priority Inversion in Event Processing', has been resolved. You can check the details in the approved PR here: https://github.com/salesforce/WorkFlowGenie/pull/40.", "utterranceID": "20260902-0-ba69d"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260902-0-ba69d"}
{"Channel": {"name": "bug-davidgarcia-WorkFlowGenie", "channelID": "ch-genie-f3ea48"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_686130c8", "timestamp": "2026-09-02T14:42:00", "text": "The root cause was a flaw in the event prioritization logic that led to misclassification of event priorities. This caused low-priority events to be processed before high-priority ones, delaying critical notifications.", "utterranceID": "20260902-1-9709f"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260902-1-9709f"}
{"Channel": {"name": "bug-davidgarcia-WorkFlowGenie", "channelID": "ch-genie-f3ea48"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_686130c8", "timestamp": "2026-09-02T14:45:00", "text": "To fix this, I updated the algorithm to correctly assess and assign priorities based on predefined criteria. I also added additional unit tests to ensure the prioritization logic works as expected in various scenarios.", "utterranceID": "20260902-2-7982b"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260902-2-7982b"}

{"Channel": {"name": "develop-georgegarcia-WorkFlowGenie", "channelID": "ch-owaix-db5d92"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_136119e9", "timestamp": "2026-05-27T22:56:00", "text": "Great points! Next, let's look at Apache Kafka's Dual Broker System for Seamless Data Streaming. This sets up a dual broker system to maintain continuous data streaming during broker upgrades or failures. Here's the link: https://github.com/apache/kafka/pull/3566", "utterranceID": "20260527-4-c5d6b"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260527-4-c5d6b"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-georgegarcia-WorkFlowGenie", "channelID": "ch-owaix-db5d92"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_c92d3e03", "timestamp": "2026-05-27T22:57:00", "text": "Kafka's dual broker system is interesting. It ensures data continuity, which is crucial for us too. We might want to explore their approach to data consistency during transitions. @Bob, any thoughts?", "utterranceID": "20260527-5-e635e"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260527-5-e635e"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-georgegarcia-WorkFlowGenie", "channelID": "ch-owaix-db5d92"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_136119e9", "timestamp": "2026-05-27T23:01:00", "text": "I agree, Julia. Kafka's method of maintaining data streaming could be very relevant for us, especially in terms of ensuring no data loss during transitions. I'll dive deeper into their implementation. 📚", "utterranceID": "20260527-6-69436"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260527-6-69436"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-georgegarcia-WorkFlowGenie", "channelID": "ch-owaix-db5d92"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_136119e9", "timestamp": "2026-05-27T23:05:00", "text": "Lastly, we have Django's Parallel ORM System for Database Transition. This feature establishes a parallel ORM system to ensure uninterrupted database operations during schema migrations. Check it out here: https://github.com/django/django/pull/3567", "utterranceID": "20260527-7-57232"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260527-7-57232"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-georgegarcia-WorkFlowGenie", "channelID": "ch-owaix-db5d92"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_18571957", "timestamp": "2026-05-27T23:08:00", "text": "Django's approach to handling database transitions is quite clever. It could give us some ideas on managing our data layer during the GraphQL transition. Maybe we can adapt some of their ORM strategies?", "utterranceID": "20260527-8-5f99c"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260527-8-5f99c"}
{"Channel": {"name": "develop-georgegarcia-WorkFlowGenie", "channelID": "ch-owaix-db5d92"}, "Message": {"User": {"userId": "eid_1e8695b6", "timestamp": "2026-05-27T23:13:00", "text": "Good call, Julia. Ensuring uninterrupted operations during transitions is key. We should definitely consider how Django manages parallel systems. @eid_136119e9, could you look into this further?", "utterranceID": "20260527-9-2dcde"}, "Reactions": []}, "ThreadReplies": [], "id": "20260527-9-2dcde"}
